WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.799
Say Alice and Bob are on opposite sides of
the universe.

00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:09.660
Entanglement means that somehow, Alice measuring
her particle affects Bob’s one even though

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.240
it’s a world away immediately.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.309
If that’s the case, then the question of
this video is “why can’t they use quantum

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:19.930
mechanics to communicate instantly”?

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:24.740
This video is very much a sequel to a video
I made about entanglement and the EPR paradox

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:25.830
so it will help a lot to watch that first.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:29.970
Actually, these videos are part of a series
on quantum mechanics so start here if you’re

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:30.970
new.

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:31.970
Back to the question.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:35.910
Let’s say Bob wants the answer to a yes
or no question that he’s asked Alice, but

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:39.220
she’s waited till she’s very far away
to think of an answer.

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:43.090
However luckily, they have an entangled pair
of particles between them.

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:44.940
Here’s a possibility.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:50.100
Alice tells Bob in advance that if he measures
his particle to be up, the answer is yes,

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.360
down is no.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:58.740
Now all Alice has to do is nonlocally influence
his particle to be up, say and then they’ve

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.690
communicated faster than light!

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:06.800
But obviously that won’t work, no matter
what she does, her own outcome is random,

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:12.030
and since Bob just gets the opposite, his
outcome just comes out completely random as

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:13.030
well.

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:17.940
So that scheme doesn’t work- but a lot of
you suggested a much more devious plan.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.890
I’ve been saying for ages that you can tell
the difference between a state in a superposition

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:27.420
of two possibilities, and one that’s merely
one of the other of these but you don’t

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.630
know which.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:30.190
Why not use that?

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:35.680
If Alice measures, the whole state collapses
and is not in a superposition anymore.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:41.920
Then surely Bob can do some interference experiment
and if he sees no interference, he knows that

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.590
Alice has done her measurement, otherwise
she hasn’t.

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:50.250
Alice can communicate what she wants by choosing
whether to measure her particle or not.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:54.790
Then they could use this scheme, and communicate
instantly across the universe!!

00:01:54.790 --> 00:02:00.350
Ok, that sounds great but obviously Quantum
mechanics has a way around that too.

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:03.710
I’ll explain it, but this is the punch line.

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:08.240
With this entangled state, you can tell they’re
in a superposition by doing some measurement

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:09.870
that shows interference.

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:14.880
However, that measurement is a measurement
where you have to interact both of the particles

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:20.490
in some way to do the measurement, and so
you need both particles in the same place.

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:25.820
If you instead only do the measurement on
one of the particles, or both particles, but

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:30.810
not together, there’s no way for you to
tell the difference between the state where

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:34.010
they’re in superposition or when they’re
not.

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:38.190
To see this, let’s look at what happens
when Bob does his measurement in the case

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:41.100
where Alice did hers already, or choose not
to.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:42.100
These are the states in either case.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:46.740
In the case where Alice has measured, his
particle really is up or down now, but he

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:47.820
doesn’t know which.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:54.000
But then say Bob does a measurement of the
left and rightness of a particle.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.771
We know that if we did this to a particle
that’s up, it will go either left or right

00:02:58.771 --> 00:03:00.520
randomly.

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:02.490
Same thing for a down particle.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:07.330
So even though we don’t know which it is,
up or down, we know it will randomly go left

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:12.599
or right, and this is the no interference
result that we talked about in some previous

00:03:12.599 --> 00:03:13.620
videos.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.530
This is kind of what we expected.

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:20.630
But what happens in the second case where
the state is still in a superposition?

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:21.810
This is a bit tricky.

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:27.260
What you need to do is write the state, in
particular Bob’s particles state, in the

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.220
left/right basis.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:31.980
Try it and then see if you can figure out
what happens.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:34.940
OK, so here’s the maths.

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:38.560
All I’ve done is subbed in these expressions.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:43.280
Then, I’m grouping up the left and right
options for Bob’s particle.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:50.720
Then you can see that both options have the
same weight, so they’re both equally likely.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:56.870
What this means is, if Bob measures his particle
for left rightness when Alice hasn’t measured,

00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:02.620
the particle is equally likely to go left
or right- which is the same as if she had

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:09.370
measured, so you can’t tell the difference.

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:14.160
But you might protest that I’ve only showed
you that this plan for faster than light communication

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:19.130
is foiled for this particular entangled state,
but what about other ones.

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:22.979
There are way way way more possible entangled
states that just this.

00:04:22.979 --> 00:04:28.129
I just use this for an example most of the
time because this state has the maximum possible

00:04:28.129 --> 00:04:32.370
amount of entanglement, but in fact most 2
particle states are entangled.

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.710
For example, this state is entangled.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:39.599
So what would we need to show that using any
entangled state is useless for faster than

00:04:39.599 --> 00:04:41.330
light communication?

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:45.849
You need to prove that no matter what Alice
does on her side, that doesn’t affect Bob’s

00:04:45.849 --> 00:04:47.180
measurement outcomes.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:52.039
For example, in either case when Alice does
or doesn’t choose to do a measurement, Bob

00:04:52.039 --> 00:04:55.449
get’s up or down with the same probability
either way.

00:04:55.449 --> 00:04:59.909
This can be proved generally, but it’s a
bit tough.

00:04:59.909 --> 00:05:03.409
Instead I’m going to get you to do it for
an example that’s quite a bit more challenging

00:05:03.409 --> 00:05:04.969
that the one I did.

00:05:04.969 --> 00:05:09.330
Prove it for this state, when Alice measures
up and down-ness, but Bob measures left-rightness.

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:15.430
I’m going to write a detailed solution in
the description, but ask me if you get confused.

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:21.290
So yes, entanglement is useless for faster
than light communication, but can we try use

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:23.050
it for something else?

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:26.770
Say Alice and Bob have lots of entangled particles
between them.

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:31.550
If they measure them all, then the outcomes
are random, but they know what the other persons

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:32.660
outcome is.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:35.969
So they share a string of random bits.

00:05:35.969 --> 00:05:41.139
Your second homework is to look up and explain
the usefulness of something called a ‘one-time

00:05:41.139 --> 00:05:42.139
pad’.

00:05:42.139 --> 00:05:47.090
Also, comment on whether or not an encryption
based on a one time pad coming from this set

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:53.439
up is secure or not if they know Eve might
be trying to intercept the particles.

00:05:53.439 --> 00:05:57.400
This is the topic for the next video.

